 ## 6. Hardware verdict (2026-07-28)
 
+> **Partly superseded by ADR-0014 (2026-08-20), `docs/adr/0014-cosuite-hardware-led-pivot.md`.**
+> Two bullets below no longer hold as written, and are kept for the record rather than edited away:
+>
+> - *"never manufactured hardware"* — still true of **manufacture**, no longer true of **assembly**.
+>   ADR-0014 commits to a prebuilt path where we assemble, image, certify and ship a unit from
+>   off-the-shelf components. No custom silicon, board, or enclosure; that part of the refusal
+>   stands, as does the supply-chain argument — ADR-0014 knowingly takes the smaller version of the
+>   exposure. Warranty, RMA and working capital move onto our balance sheet, which §12's
+>   partner-supplied Spark tier had deliberately kept off it.
+> - *"the seam is built, the local implementation is not"* — superseded by
+>   `feat/appliance-model-plane`. `packages/shared/src/model-plane.ts` makes the base URL
+>   configurable, and `startup-guard.ts:85` makes an off-box model plane a startup failure under
+>   `CORTEX_DEPLOYMENT=appliance`. **The branch is not merged to `main`, and the vLLM path has
+>   never been run end to end** (`docs/handoff/PILOT-DOGFOOD-RUNBOOK.md:362`, tracker #130). Until
+>   it has, claims stay in the enforcement form, never the performance form.
+>
+> The mic-hardware verdict immediately below is **not** superseded and remains in force.
+
 - **Mic hardware: no.** Reverses two documented strategic constraints ("no proprietary recorder, ever"; "no custom hardware"). Platform notes give speaker identity free; a room mic gives raw audio, and diarization error becomes ACL-inheritance error (unknown #9). Firmware/certification/supply chain kills two-founder velocity. Consent friction.
 - **Local compute node: yes — as software appliance, never manufactured hardware.** "Node in a box" = one image (docker-compose / NixOS) running the whole stack incl. vLLM open-weight generation on the customer's own mini-PC/VM. One command up, default-deny, brain never touches the internet. If demand proves: partner with a NUC-class vendor; never manufacture.
 - Easy setup is a **software** property; shipping hardware makes setup harder (procurement, IT approval, logistics).
