 ## Changelog
 
+- 2026-08-20 — ADR-0014 (Co-Suite hardware-led pivot): the product becomes a box. **Co-Suite** is the machine plus the stack that ships on it; **Cortex** narrows to the memory engine inside it; Conscience stays the governance plane; Cornea stays internal-only. Two purchase paths against one signed image — prebuilt by us (assembled, imaged, certified from commodity parts, build-to-order, no speculative inventory) and bring-your-own-box meeting a spec that does not yet exist (#119). Assembly, not design: no custom silicon, board, or enclosure. Mass ingest demoted from the pitch to a benefit of owning the box. Moat stated as by-combination — "nobody has assembled this", never "nobody has thought of this". §12's retention posture retained and load-bearing, because the code now sits on hardware we handed over. **§6 and §12 amended — see the superseding note in §6.** Four risks move onto our balance sheet that §12's partner-supplied Spark tier kept off it: supply/lead time (#120), working capital (#121), warranty/RMA (#123), assembly and certification (#124). Drivers: the original problem set, unchanged; plus per-token compute cost; plus information security stated as *locality* rather than only access control — an argument that is false today and only becomes true on a customer-owned box. **Two gaps recorded rather than papered over:** the compute-cost driver has no cost model, unit economics or token arithmetic anywhere in the repo, and there is no hardware price, spec, margin or lead time. **The ADR in this repo is a reconstruction** from the twenty tracker issues citing it; the original was authored outside version control and has never been committed. Status stays `RECONSTRUCTED DRAFT` until reconciled.
+
 - 2026-08-07 — ADR-0011 (services and pricing posture): take the FDE money, not the FDE identity. Charge implementation explicitly ($25-75K, the band comparables establish) rather than internalising it as Palantir does — affordable at their scale, fatal at ours. Fast to first value, long to full value: Palantir's own bootcamp is 1-5 days to a use case on real data, then 8-12 weeks to production, which is §7's "setup speed sells the demo" already. Every FDE task gated on the compile test ("useful for the next 20 customers"), F-Prime's 30-40%-of-deployments threshold adopted as an operating metric from customer one. **Two assumptions previously treated as known are recorded as unevidenced**: that regulated buyers prefer high-touch deployment (no study found at all), and that services work compiles into product (correlation only, with a published counter-argument). The ratchet is now commercially load-bearing, not only technically — it is the whole difference between a software multiple and a services one.
 - 2026-08-06 — ADR-0010 (ingest auth strategy): BYOA for wave 1, rented for the tail, `cortex install --org` as the department Day-0 product rather than a convenience wrapper. Google sequenced before Slack. Public app suite rejected for the wedge (shared client id = shared blast radius; marketplace review per platform; inverts the regulated pitch) and left open as a distribution question. DM ingest and org-wide overwatch rejected by design. Slack mechanics verified against current docs — see the ADR's Evidence section, including two items that need Slack directly.
 
